-- the numeric encoding. The four worked compounds each cost a type, a rank, an element table, a bond
-- table and a generator for every symmetry, written out atom by atom. That is the right form for a case
-- to be read and checked by hand, and the wrong form for a table of hundreds. Here a compound is data
-- over the atoms 0 to n-1: the symmetries are built from two combinators rather than written out, and a
-- compound costs a few lines of numbers. The two encodings are independent, and where they cover the
-- same molecule they are made to agree.
--
-- the exchange of two lists of atoms, position by position: the i-th of one for the i-th of the other.
-- This is one combinator because every exchange in these molecules exchanges whole groups -- a methyl
-- for a methyl, an arm for an arm -- and carries their hydrogens along.
nat-xch : List Nat → List Nat → Nat → Nat
nat-xch xs ys x = case xs of
  nil → x
  cons a r → (case ys of nil → x; cons b s → (case eqb x a of true → b; false → (case eqb x b of true → a; false → nat-xch r s x)))
